Dysphagia
Palliative & End of Life
Severe choking/coughing with meals, Sensation of food sticking, Recurrent aspiration pneumonia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-023
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe choking episode with cyanosis during a meal, followed by ongoing respiratory distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient experienced a severe choking episode on solid food, resulting in pronounced coughing, gagging, and transient perioral cyanosis. Although the major airway blockage cleared, the patient remains highly distressed with a wet, gurgling voice and localized retrosternal pain. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to rule out a residual foreign body, assess for acute aspiration pneumonitis, and establish an immediate safe feeding protocol prior to morning to prevent a fatal choking event.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I tried to swallow a piece of chicken, and it just stopped right in my throat. I couldn't breathe, and now every time I swallow my own spit I start coughing."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two months, the patient has developed severe, profound difficulty explicitly initiating a safe, effective, and functionally coordinated swallow. There is a distinct, highly distressing sensation of food explicitly sticking precisely in the throat or upper retrosternal chest. The spouse notes frequent, severe choking, pronounced coughing, or explicit gagging strictly during or immediately after swallowing food or specific liquids. There is a history of recurrent, severe aspiration pneumonia strictly secondary to these deeply impaired functional swallow mechanics. The patient reports noticeable regurgitation of previously swallowed, undigested food back into the oral cavity. There is unintentional, rapid weight loss and severe malnutrition directly resulting from an absolute inability to maintain adequate oral intake. The spouse notes drooling and a severe inability to effectively manage endogenous oral secretions. Prolonged meal times characterized by extreme effort and severe psychological distress are standard.
Functional Status: Weakened, highly anxious regarding eating, requiring constant supervision during meals.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of fatal aspiration or asphyxiation; severe caregiver trauma from witnessing the acute choking episode.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Patient is sitting upright, visibly anxious, frequently clearing throat, with a noticeably wet, gurgling voice immediately after drinking a sip of water.
Physical Exam: Respiratory auscultation reveals transmitted upper airway sounds and coarse crackles at the right lung base, highly suspicious for fresh aspiration. Neurological exam reveals absent gag reflex, poor palatal elevation, and profound bulbar weakness. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the observed severe choking/coughing with meals (reproduced with a sip of water), the reported sensation of food sticking, and the history of recurrent aspiration pneumonia.
Environmental Observations: The dinner plate remains on the table with tough, dry meats (chicken breast), which are highly inappropriate for a patient with profound bulbar palsy.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is currently on large, solid oral tablets for cardiac and palliative conditions, posing a massive ongoing choking hazard.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dysphagia - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Aspiration Event)
Differentials:

Complete foreign body airway obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely at present, as the patient is phonating (albeit with a wet voice) and moving air bilaterally, indicating the major obstruction has passed or was cleared.

Acute myocardial infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the retrosternal discomfort explicitly tying directly to the act of swallowing the bolus of food, and the absence of radiating pain or ECG changes (if available).

Esophageal malignancy (new) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary acute cause; the dysphagia is oropharyngeal (bulbar) given the immediate choking, wet voice, and difficulty initiating the swallow, rather than a strictly mechanical lower blockage.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient has suffered a severe, acute aspiration event secondary to progressive, profound end-stage oropharyngeal dysphagia. The objective clinical evidence—a wet, gurgling voice, tachypnea, right basal crackles, and an absent gag reflex—confirms that airway protection is completely compromised. Attempting any further unmodified oral intake or solid medication administration tonight would place the patient at extreme risk of a fatal choking event or massive chemical pneumonitis. Urgent after-hours medical intervention was essential to definitively halt solid oral intake, assess the lungs, and prescribe immediate medication modifications to keep the patient safe until a formal speech pathology review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to remain strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM) for all solid foods and thin liquids tonight. Prescribed thickener for water and converted essential evening medications to liquid or crushable forms to be given with thickened yogurt/puree.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the need to eat or drink (trigger), you must only consume foods that are completely pureed and liquids that are thickened to a syrup consistency (behavior). This slows the flow of the food, giving your weakened throat muscles time to close the airway, completely preventing the terrifying choking spells (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community speech pathology for a formal swallowing assessment and modified diet plan tomorrow.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure an oral suction machine is available if secretion pooling worsens.
Follow-up: GP review in 24 hours to monitor for the development of aspiration pneumonia requiring antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a high fever, severe shortness of breath at rest, chest pain, or if another choking episode occurs where the patient cannot breathe or turns blue, the spouse must perform back blows/abdominal thrusts and call 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The muscles in the throat are permanently weakened by the illness; swallowing will not return to normal. The goal is completely avoiding choking.
Clinical Course: Without strict adherence to pureed and thickened diets, severe lung infections are inevitable.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to eat any solid foods, dry meats, or thin liquids (like plain water or tea) under any circumstances. All medications must be crushed and mixed with a spoonful of thick yogurt.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The nerves controlling your swallowing are failing. When you swallow thin water or solid food, the trapdoor over your lungs doesn't close fast enough, and the food drops straight into your lungs, causing the choking and the wet voice. We must switch everything to a thick, smooth consistency so it slides down the correct pipe slowly.
Actionable Steps: Sit completely upright at 90 degrees for all meals and remain upright for 30 minutes afterward. Tuck your chin down to your chest right before you swallow to help close the airway.

